s TD equity \u2014 LV surrender 0.44 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone KC throw it (56% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Rashee Rice</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><p>In the red zone LV throw it (59% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Tre Tucker</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to KC \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>KC \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>KC stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>50%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> (15 carries, 65 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though KC's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Rashee Rice</strong> projects for <strong>9 targets and 77 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Travis Kelce</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 51 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>LV \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>LV are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (46% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 LV project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Ashton Jeanty</strong>'s rushing projection (17 carries, 64 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Tre Tucker</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, LV funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>6 targets for 44 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Brock Bowers</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 49 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 6-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"KC","col":"#FF4D57","players":[{"name":"Patrick Mahomes","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":248.73,"book":null,"over_th":234.87,"under_th":261.04,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.76,"book":null,"over_th":1.05,"under_th":1.44,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge"